# Typed RPC records and enums

Odroe generates wire codecs for project-local named-record typedefs and directly
declared enums. Records and enums stay typed in Flutter and server code; only
the RPC boundary turns a record into a JSON object and an enum into its case
name string. No annotation, `build_runner`, `toJson`, reflection, or global
adapter registry is involved.

This is the smallest useful domain model for the generated full-stack product:

```dart
// lib/posts.dart
typedef Post = ({int id, String title});
typedef CreatePost = ({String title});
typedef PostPage = ({List<Post> items, int? nextCursor});
enum PostSort { newest, oldest }
typedef ListPostsInput = ({
  int? cursor,
  List<int> ids,
  int limit,
  PostSort sort,
});
```

Keep that file client-safe. It must not import SQLite, D1, `dart:io`, a server
host, or application secrets. Flutter and the server import the same types.

## Import the shared model with a prefix

A route server file imports the model with an explicit prefix. The prefix tells
the route compiler which library owns the client-visible type and prevents a
server-only declaration from leaking into generated client code.

The record typedef and enum declaration must live directly in that imported
source file. The compiler does not follow `part` directives or re-exports to
discover them. If `posts.dart` only exports `post_models.dart`, import
`post_models.dart` itself with the prefix instead.

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database.dart';
import 'package:odroe/server.dart';

import '../../posts.dart' as models;
import '../../posts_database.dart';
import 'route.dart' as definition;

final route = definition.route.server();

final listPosts = ServerFunction<models.ListPostsInput, models.PostPage>(
  id: 'posts.list',
  method: HttpMethod.get,
  handler: listPostPage,
);

final createPost = ServerFunction<models.CreatePost, models.Post>(
  id: 'posts.create',
  method: HttpMethod.post,
  handler: (context) {
    final title = context.data.title.trim();
    if (title.isEmpty) {
      throw const HttpError(400, 'Post title is required.');
    }
    return postQueries
        .insert(posts, <SqlAssignment>[posts.title.set(title)])
        .returning(posts.projection)
        .one(context.request.read(databaseKey));
  },
);
```

`listPostPage` is the bounded database handler shown in
[Full-stack vertical slice](/docs/tutorials/full-stack#add-page-and-create-functions):
it validates `limit` as `1..50`, caps the ID filter, applies the strict ID
cursor predicate, and reads `limit + 1`. Generated typing does not replace
application validation or a database provider's bind parameter budget.

`odroe generate` resolves `models.Post`, `models.PostPage`,
`models.CreatePost`, `models.PostSort`, and `models.ListPostsInput`, reads the
record fields and enum declaration, then emits the four transformations needed
by one typed call:

```text
Flutter value
  → generated input encoder
  → JSON object / enum name
  → generated server input decoder
  → handler value

handler value
  → generated server output encoder
  → JSON object / enum name
  → generated client output decoder
  → Flutter value
```

The same output codec is applied to each stream item. Collection codecs
recurse through nullable values, `List`, `Set`, `Iterable`, `Map<String, T>`,
and named-record fields. `ListPostsInput.ids`, `ListPostsInput.sort`, and
`PostPage.items` therefore preserve their Dart types instead of becoming
`dynamic` or failing as unregistered values at runtime.

## Keep enums typed on both sides

Generated code transports `PostSort.newest` as the JSON string `"newest"` and
decodes it through `PostSort.values.byName`. It deliberately reads Dart's
declaration name even when an enhanced enum defines its own field named `name`.
Enum indexes, `toString()`, and application display labels never enter the wire
contract.

Unknown or non-string enum input is rejected with HTTP 400 before the function
handler runs. An unknown enum in a successful response becomes
`RpcProtocolException` on the client. The same rules apply when the enum is a
direct function type, a non-generic typedef alias, nested in a supported
collection or record, nullable, or used as a stream item.

## Call records directly from Flutter

Generated references expose the original Dart types:

```dart
final created = await generated.routes.posts.createPost(
  rpc,
  (title: 'Typed from Flutter'),
);

final page = await generated.routes.posts.listPosts(
  rpc,
  (
    cursor: null,
    ids: <int>[42, 44],
    limit: 20,
    sort: PostSort.newest,
  ),
);

print(created.id);
print(page.items.first.title);
print(page.nextCursor);
```

There is no application-owned map or cast in this path. A malformed object is
rejected before the server handler starts. A successful response whose shape
does not match the generated output contract becomes `RpcProtocolException` on
the client instead of exposing an incidental cast error.

## Know the supported boundary

Generated codecs intentionally cover a narrow, inspectable shape:

- a project-local typedef declared directly in the same source file that the
  route server imports with a prefix; `part` and re-export discovery are not
  supported;
- a project-local enum declared directly in that source file, including a
  non-generic alias chain;
- named fields only;
- non-generic record aliases;
- use-site and field nullability, plus `List`, `Set`, `Iterable`, and
  `Map<String, T>` around supported values;
- scalar values already supported by `Serializer`, including `DateTime`,
  `Duration`, `Uri`, `BigInt`, and `Uint8List`.

Positional records, generic record aliases, recursive aliases, function values,
and maps with non-string keys are rejected during route generation. The
same-file rule does not add generated support for a nominal class, including
one declared beside the typedef. Dependency enums, imported indirect enums,
nominal classes, and enum values hidden behind `Object` or `dynamic` remain
application-owned and use a matching `SerializationAdapter` on both sides when
they must cross the wire. Odroe does not scan dependency source, infer
constructors, or generate `fromJson` for classes.

For a directly declared project enum, the generated codec is authoritative and
runs before `Serializer`; a custom enum adapter will no longer receive that
value. Keep adapters for dependency or opaque values, not for the same local
enum contract.

Put `?` on the function use site or a record field, such as `models.Post?`.
A typedef whose record target is itself nullable is rejected because that alias
cannot name the promoted non-null record needed by generated field access.

## Treat fields and cases as a protocol

The function ID, record field names, and enum case names are wire contracts.
Renaming or removing a case, renaming a field, changing its type, or changing
nullability can break an older client even when the Dart declaration still
looks small. Reordering enum cases does not change the wire value. Adding a case
still requires a compatibility decision because an older decoder cannot know
the new name. Use a new function ID or a coordinated client/server release for
a breaking shape change.

The generated wire value is an object keyed by field name. Its key set must
match the record exactly: missing fields and unknown fields are rejected, even
when a missing field would otherwise be nullable. That fail-closed rule prevents
two deployed shapes from drifting silently. Do not depend on field order and
do not place secrets in a record merely because the type is shared. Payload and
response-frame byte limits still apply after encoding; keep list RPCs bounded
with a page record or use streaming when the product needs a stream.

Run the compiler explicitly after changing a shared wire type:

```sh
dart run odroe generate
dart analyze --fatal-infos
```

